                <h2>High Reach vs. High Conversions: Why They Are Not the Same</h2>
<p>Many campaign managers have experienced this: a creative with 3% CTR and sub-$0.50 CPC looks like a goldmine—until three days later, when conversions are near zero and ROAS drops to 0.3.</p>
<p>The issue? <strong>High reach and high sales require completely different evaluation frameworks.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Q1: CTR Is High but Conversion Rate Is Low—What's Wrong?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: The wrong traffic is coming through.</strong></p>
<p>High CTR means the creative is eye-catching, but it may not be attracting your target users. Two common causes:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Creative tone vs. audience expectation mismatch</strong>: A flashy game CG ad pulls in casual viewers who click but never pay.</li>
<li><strong>Landing page disconnect</strong>: The ad promises "free trial," but users hit a paywall immediately after clicking.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Track post-click behavior</strong>: time on page, scroll depth, add-to-cart rate. These metrics reveal whether your creative is pulling in the right people.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q2: When Should You Kill a High-Performing Creative?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: When CPA rises for three consecutive days with no external explanation.</strong></p>
<p>Creative fatigue is natural, but the decision to pause should be cost-driven, not volume-driven.</p>
<p>Key criteria:</p>
<ul>
<li>CPA is 40%+ higher than the peak</li>
<li>Add-to-cart rate is declining</li>
<li>Frequency has saturated (4-5+ impressions per user)</li>
</ul>
<p>A utility app's approach works: <strong>eliminate the bottom 20% of creatives daily, refresh the full test pool weekly.</strong> Better to kill a potential winner than let a dying creative burn budget.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q3: Is the Creative Actually Selling, or Just Riding a Trend?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: Turn off the creative and test with the audience alone.</strong></p>
<p>Swap your creative for a plain product-focused image. If organic conversions drop more than 30%, your high performer was riding creative appeal, not product-market fit.</p>
<p>Another test: <strong>check performance across different audience segments</strong>. A truly selling creative shows stable conversion gaps between broad and targeted audiences. Wild fluctuations mean the creative's power depends on the specific audience, not the content itself.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q4: What's the Right Way to Run A/B Tests?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: Change only one variable at a time, and test long enough.</strong></p>
<p>Running five creative versions simultaneously doesn't tell you anything useful. Correct approach:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Test Dimension</th>
<th>Only Change One</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Copy</td>
<td>Version A vs B, same visual</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Visual</td>
<td>Version A vs B, same copy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CTA Button</td>
<td>"Download Now" vs "Try Free"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Audience</td>
<td>Same creative, two audience packs</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Minimum test duration: <strong>5-7 days</strong> to cover a full audience cycle. Cold-start data from the first two days has no statistical value.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Q5: Creative Lifecycles Are Shrinking—How Do You Keep Up?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: Build a 3-2-1 creative pipeline.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>3主力素材</strong> (3 core creatives): running stable volume</li>
<li><strong>2测试素材</strong> (2 test creatives): in A/B phase</li>
<li><strong>1储备素材</strong> (1 backup creative): tested and ready to launch</li>
</ul>
<p>When a core creative fades, promote the backup immediately. New creative turnaround should stay under 5 business days.</p>
<p>One e-commerce team's result: shortening creative lifecycle from 14 days to 7 days actually improved overall ROAS by 15%. <strong>Proactive replacement before audience fatigue hits is more cost-effective than rescue attempts after a creative dies.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Q6: How Should Account Structure Support Creative Strategy?</h2>
<p><strong>Answer: Structure adapts to creatives, not the other way around.</strong></p>
<p>Common rookie mistake: stuffing all creatives into one ad set, resulting in contaminated data. Recommended structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One creative per ad set</strong> (or two for controlled A/B)</li>
<li><strong>Campaigns split by conversion goal</strong>: acquisition vs. retargeting, different creative packages</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic budget shifting</strong>: daily, allocate 60% of budget to the top 20% performing creatives</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Creative Review Checklist: Is Your Creative Actually Selling?</h2>
<p>Run through this at every review meeting:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] CPA vs. target gap—how much?</li>
<li>[ ] Post-click metrics (engagement, scroll, add-to-cart)—passing?</li>
<li>[ ] Audience segment performance variance—within range?</li>
<li>[ ] Creative lifecycle—entering decline?</li>
<li>[ ] Backup creatives—ready for replacement?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Bottom line</strong>: High-reach creatives solve volume problems. High-converting creatives solve ROAS problems. Don't evaluate both with the same metric framework. Run high-reach creatives to build volume, then let high-converting creatives do the heavy lifting. The combination is how you win on Meta.</p>
